    hook:{title:"Three countries, three stages", body:"In Niger, families are large and the population is growing fast. In Japan, there are more deaths than births and the population is shrinking. South Africa sits in between: births are falling but still higher than deaths. The <strong>Demographic Transition Model</strong> explains how countries move through these stages as they develop."},
    intro:"The <strong>Demographic Transition Model (DTM)</strong> shows changes in <strong>birth rate</strong> and <strong>death rate</strong> over time. <strong>Stage 1</strong>: both high, slow growth. <strong>Stage 2</strong>: death rate falls (better health care, clean water, food) while births stay high — <strong>rapid growth</strong>. <strong>Stage 3</strong>: birth rate falls (education, contraception, urbanisation, women working). <strong>Stage 4</strong>: both low, stable population. <strong>Stage 5</strong>: birth rate below death rate — population declines. <strong>Natural increase (%) = (birth rate − death rate) ÷ 10</strong>, when rates are per 1 000 people.",
